  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nHigh pressure aloft will expand and build into the weekend. This \nmeans high temperatures will continue and increase over the next \nfew days. \n\nHigh temperatures for the coastal areas today will be a few degrees \ncooler than yesterdays highs, while high temperatures in the \nmountains and Inland Empire will warm 5 to 10 degrees. Thursday, \nhighs will warm 5 to locally 10 degrees in the valleys and in the \ncoastal mountain slopes with little to no change for high \ntemperatures Friday and Saturday. This will bring seasonal \ntemperatures 5 to 10 degrees above average for most of the area \nThursday through Saturday.\n\nElevated overnight lows will offer little to no reprieve from the \ndaytime heat. Coastal areas and inland valleys will see overnight \nlows predominately in the 70s, upper 80s to upper 90s in the low \ndeserts, and mid 70s to mid 80s for the high deserts.\n\nThese high afternoon temperatures and elevated lows will bring \nwidespread moderate HeatRisk to most of the area with major HeatRisk \nin the Inland Empire, San Diego mountains and south deserts today, \nand to include the high deserts Friday through Sunday. Major to \nextreme HeatRisk indicates that anyone may be negatively impacted by \nthe heat if the proper precautions are not taken. Make sure to stay \nhydrated, stay cool, avoid heavy activity and direct sunlight and \ncheck on the elderly, children and pets.\n\nCoastal low clouds are expected to be patchy in nature and stick \nclose to the coast through Saturday. A slight increase in \nmonsoonal moisture will create mid-level clouds over the mountains\nThursday and Friday with a 10-20% chance for light rain in the \nmountains for San Bernardino and Riverside Counties.\n\nHigh temperatures will continue into early next week with some \nslight cooling for daily high and low temperatures by mid-week. The \nECMWF and GFS show high pressure aloft returning by the end of next \nweek that is as strong or possibly stronger.",
